rked in place, and Next stop to move on.)\n\n- <kbd>‹</kbd> <kbd>›</kbd> at the top, or <kbd>←</kbd> <kbd>→</kbd> while reading, step from stop\n  to stop. **Next stop ›**, in the text under the current stop, does the same, and at the end of a\n  pass becomes **More detail ›**. **‹ Previous stop**, on the left under it, goes back one.\n- A line above the stops, written by AI, maps the pass you are on: what it goes through, and in\n  what order, so the jumps between passages make sense. It never says what the piece found.\n- Some stops have a short question above the quote, written by AI, to read it with: what the quote\n  refers to, what it settles, or why it matters — never what it found. A stop whose quote stands on\n  its own has none.\n- A deeper pass has its own new stops and may keep earlier ones where the new ones lean on them.\n  When a route has any, small dots under each stop’s number show which passes it is in, shallowest\n  first: more than one filled, and you may have read it already.\n- Under the current stop, a card gathers what other modes have already found there: **Terms it\n  uses** and **Ideas it bears on**.\n- The dot beside each row shows how far through the article that stop is, so you can see the route\n  jump about.\n- **Reading for:** at the top is what you said you want from this article; the route is planned\n  around it. **Edit** opens it in place: say what you are skimming for and press **Plan the route\n  for this**, and the route is planned again around it. It is the same sentence as the article’s\n  purpose in Metadata, so other modes use it too. With nothing said yet, the box is already there.\n- The route prefers shorter passages, so a skim does not stall on a paragraph-long quote; a long\n  one is kept for the deeper passes unless it is the only one that makes its point."
+  "body": "## In short\n\nSkim picks out a handful of passages which, read on their own, give you a good sense of what the\npiece is about. Think of it as an alternative to a summary that keeps you in the article: every stop\nis a passage from the article, or one of its figures, in place, sometimes with a short question to\nread it with. Along the way it\nshows what Glossary and Ideas have already found at each stop, such as an unfamiliar term explained.\nGo round quickly with a few stops, then again in more detail, then again in more.\n\n(Picture: Pressing the right arrow twice: the outlined passage in the article moves to the next stop each time, with Next stop and the next cue beneath it. Caption: Each press of → or Next stop moves to the next stop, and the article scrolls to it.)\n\n## How it works\n\nA route through the piece's quotes, a little deeper each time round.\n\nA short model call over the article's Quotes and its key Ideas — never the rest of its prose — puts the Quotes in an order and gives each a depth, so each pass covers as many of the Ideas as the quotes reach, shaped by your profile if you have one. When there are no Quotes or Ideas yet, they are made first; finding the Ideas is the longer part.\n\n## When to use it\n\nWhen you need the shape of a paper fast but do not want a summary in place of the text: every stop\nis a passage from the article, in place, in context. **Gist** is a handful of stops, **More** about a\ndozen, **Most** a larger share of the piece; each walks more stops than the one before. The order is\nplanned for you — the results first, say, then a quick look at the methods — not the article’s. Only whoever added the article can plan a\nroute; visitors to a shared article can walk one already planned.\n\n## Reading it\n\n(Picture: A Skim stop: the route’s stops on the left with the current one open, and its passage highlighted in the article on the right, with Next stop below. Caption: A stop on the route: the question to read it with, the passage marked in place, and Next stop to move on.)\n\n- <kbd>‹</kbd> <kbd>›</kbd> at the top, or <kbd>←</kbd> <kbd>→</kbd> while reading, step from stop\n  to stop. **Next stop ›**, in the text under the current stop, does the same, and at the end of a\n  pass becomes **More detail ›**. **‹ Previous stop**, on the left under it, goes back one.\n- A line above the stops, written by AI, maps the pass you are on: what it goes through, and in\n  what order, so the jumps between passages make sense. It never says what the piece found.\n- Some stops have a short question above the quote, written by AI, to read it with: what the quote\n  refers to, what it settles, or why it matters — never what it found. A stop whose quote stands on\n  its own has none.\n- On a paper with figures, a stop can be a figure or a table instead of a passage, where looking at\n  it helps with the passages around it. Its row shows the caption, and a line above it, written by\n  AI, says why it is there and what to look at — never what it shows the result to be. Stepping to\n  it brings the figure into view. Visitors to a shared article walk the passages only.\n- A deeper pass has its own new stops and may keep earlier ones where the new ones lean on them.\n  When a route has any, small dots under each stop’s number show which passes it is in, shallowest\n  first: more than one filled, and you may have read it already.\n- Under the current stop, a card gathers what other modes have already found there: **Terms it\n  uses** and **Ideas it bears on**.\n- The dot beside each row shows how far through the article that stop is, so you can see the route\n  jump about.\n- **Reading for:** at the top is what you said you want from this article; the route is planned\n  around it. **Edit** opens it in place: say what you are skimming for and press **Plan the route\n  for this**, and the route is planned again around it. It is the same sentence as the article’s\n  purpose in Metadata, so other